WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.720
When people living with diabetes get sick, the right fluid choice

2
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.800
depends on their blood glucose level.

3
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.400
So, for example, if their blood glucose levels are above 10 millimoles per

4
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.380
liter, we should be looking at more carb-free fluids.

5
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:30.220
So things like water, soda water, unsweetened

6
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.820
tea, that type of thing. The choice of

7
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:37.660
fluid is up to the person. There's not one right

8
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:37.940
way.

9
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.449
But if the glucose is below 10, and particularly if they're not

10
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:46.330
eating or there's vomiting and diarrhea and those sorts of

11
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.460
issues at the same time, it would be best to have a

12
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.440
carb-containing fluid. So examples could be

13
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.180
fruit juice or a lemonade, not a sugar-free one, a

14
00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.100
sugary lemonade,

15
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.080
or milk is another option.

16
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.040
Now, people living with diabetes might be asking you questions

17
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.970
like, "Where does oral rehydration solutions fit into

18
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:15.910
sick day management?"

19
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.080
Now, taking you back a couple of steps, oral rehydration solution, or

20
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.030
ORS as we'll shorten it for today, is a specific formulation

21
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.820
of water, electrolytes, and a small amount of glucose.

22
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:32.160
Now, there's actually a really common misconception that people living with

23
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.040
diabetes can't have an ORS because of that tiny amount of

24
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.880
glucose, when in fact, that glucose in the

25
00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.360
special formulation that it comes in is needed for quicker

26
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.260
rehydration.

27
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.220
Now, it might sound strange that we actually need a little bit of glucose,

28
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:52.720
but the glucose in ORS activates the sodium glucose

29
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.710
co-transporter 1, so the SGLT1, creating an

30
00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.100
osmotic gradient that what it does is it pulls water into the

31
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.680
bloodstream and intestines more effectively than what water would

32
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.160
alone. And so this is exactly why ORS is

33
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.021
recommended in Australian guidelines for people living with diabetes

34
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.560
who have vomiting or diarrhea and other sorts of viral illnesses.

35
00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:25.360
The World Health Organization recommends ORS to

36
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.320
contain about 1.35 grams of glucose per

37
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:33.280
100 mils, so regardless of a sachet or tablet or ready-made

38
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:37.160
product, which is actually far less than everyday foods.

39
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:41.420
Just as a quick comparison, you'd need to drink about a

40
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:45.500
liter of those types of ORS to be equivalent to eating one

41
00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:49.340
piece of bread. So it's quite a large volume, really,

42
00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.740
to be half a sandwich.

43
00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.740
There are some formulations as well which are considered carb-free, so that means

44
00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:00.500
that they can be used even when those glucose levels are getting quite

45
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.900
high. And it's also really important to

46
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.740
consider that not all ORS are made the

47
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.720
same. So ORS are quite different from things like sports

48
00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.380
drinks. Now, these can be isotonic and

49
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.340
also contain a fair bit more sugar than your WHO,

50
00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.380
or your World Health Organization, aligned ORS ones do.

51
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.120
And these are definitely considered carb-containing, which isn't appropriate for

52
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.280
rehydration in every context.

53
00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.940
ORS are great products to use in sick day management

54
00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.760
for anyone living with diabetes. They're really, really great in certain

55
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.560
populations such as older individuals, or people who are

56
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:44.940
struggling with their fluid intake because of vomiting or diarrhea,

57
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.820
and also those at risk of rapid dehydration that might have other

58
00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:50.780
comorbidities.
